<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/expert/generative-models">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Generative models <strong>learn a data distribution well enough to sample new examples from
    it</strong> — faces, art, music, code, molecules. They are the AI family behind image
    generation, music synthesis, drug design and every text generator. The shift they represent
    is conceptual before it is industrial: discriminative models learn a boundary between
    categories (is this a cat?), while generative models learn the <em>shape of the data
    itself</em> — enough of cats to produce one that never existed. That is a much harder
    target (you must get every detail of the distribution right, not just the decision
    boundary) and a much more useful one, because a good generative model is simultaneously an
    artist, a simulator, a data-augmentation engine and a hypothesis generator for science.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything in this chapter stands on one substrate — deep learning — so the chapter starts
    there: what deep networks actually compute, why depth unlocked generation, and how the
    three great generative families (adversarial, diffusion, autoregressive) each answer the
    same question, "how do you sample from a distribution you cannot write down?", with
    different machinery.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the generative landscape: families, latent spaces, steering, open questions" widget="equationStep" config={genCfg} />

  <h2>Deep learning: the substrate generators run on</h2>
  <p>
    A deep neural network is a stack of layers, each applying learned weights and a nonlinearity,
    trained by <strong>backpropagation</strong>: make a prediction, measure the error, compute
    the gradient of that error with respect to every weight (the chain rule, applied
    systematically backwards), nudge each weight downhill, repeat over millions of examples.
    Depth is the point: each layer builds features from the layer below — edges into textures,
    textures into parts, parts into objects — so the network learns a <em>hierarchy of
    representations</em> rather than a flat mapping. The moment the field flipped is easy to
    date: AlexNet at ImageNet 2012 cut top-5 classification error from 26.2% to 15.3% in one
    year by combining depth, GPUs and data; error fell under 3% by 2015, matching human
    raters. Generative models inherit three things from this substrate: gradient descent as the
    universal learning rule, learned representation hierarchies as the medium, and — crucially —
    <strong>scaling laws</strong>: network quality improves predictably (smoothly, measurably)
    as parameters, data and compute grow together, which is what turned research demos into an
    industry and made "we trained it bigger" a strategy with a published price-performance
    curve.
  </p>

  <h2>Two families of image generators: adversarial and diffusion</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>GANs</strong> (Goodfellow et al., 2014) answer the sampling problem with a game: a
    <em>generator</em> network maps random noise to images, a <em>discriminator</em> learns to
    tell generated from real, and each improves against the other until — at equilibrium —
    generated images are indistinguishable from real ones. The mechanism produced startling
    results (StyleGAN faces, this-person-does-not-exist) and one chronic pathology:
    <strong>mode collapse</strong>, where the generator finds a few outputs that reliably fool
    the discriminator and abandons the rest of the distribution — producing a forger with one
    good trick. <strong>Diffusion models</strong> (Ho et al., 2020; the family behind DALL·E 3,
    Stable Diffusion, Midjourney) took the opposite route: <em>forward</em>, destroy an image by
    adding Gaussian noise over hundreds of steps; then train a network to reverse one step of
    that corruption; at generation time, start from pure noise and run the learned denoiser
    (typically 20–50 steps, with modern samplers) to arrive at a coherent image. There is no
    adversary to fool, training is stable, and the step-by-step refinement gives an
    architecture where text conditioning plugs in cleanly. That is why diffusion effectively
    won: by 2022–23 it was the default for essentially every serious image generator.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "diffusion models just denoise images they have seen." The
    denoiser is trained to predict noise on <em>corrupted</em> inputs, and generation samples
    new trajectories through image space from random starts — the model learns the gradient of
    the data distribution, not an album. The related claim "it's all memorisation" is testable,
    and the tests (Carlini et al., 2023) found real but rare memorisation — concentrated in
    duplicated training images — while typical outputs are novel compositions of learned
    regularities. Both the hype and the dismissal miss the measured truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Autoregressive generation: language, one token at a time</h2>
  <p>
    Text generation runs on a third mechanism: <strong>autoregression</strong>. A transformer
    (self-attention letting every token attend to every previous one) is trained on one
    deceptively simple objective — predict the next token, given everything so far — and
    generates by repeatedly sampling a token, appending it, and predicting again. GPT-3
    (2020), with 175 billion parameters trained on roughly 300 billion tokens, showed the
    objective's secret: doing next-token prediction extraordinarily well forces the model to
    learn grammar, facts, styles and even multi-step reasoning patterns, because all of them
    are useful for the prediction. Sampling parameters shape the output: temperature scales
    randomness (low temperature is cautious and repetitive; high is adventurous), top-p
    truncates the tail of unlikely tokens. Scaling laws (Kaplan 2020; Hoffmann's Chinchilla
    2022) made the craft quantitative — for a fixed compute budget there is an optimal ratio
    of data to parameters (roughly 20 tokens per parameter) — which is how labs decide
    training plans worth hundreds of millions of dollars before the first GPU spins.
  </p>

  <h2>Latent space: the geometry of learned meaning</h2>
  <p>
    Generative models do not store images; they store a compressed coordinate system — the
    <strong>latent space</strong> — where each point decodes to an output and (the vital
    property) nearby points decode to similar concepts. Variational autoencoders (VAEs, 2013)
    trained explicit encoder–decoder pairs to build such spaces with structure imposed by
    design; diffusion systems like Stable Diffusion run in a compressed latent space (a
    512 × 512 pixel image lives in a 64 × 64 latent — an 8 × 8 per-side compression — which is
    why consumer GPUs can run them at all). The geometry is queryable: interpolate between two
    faces and the video passes through plausible intermediate faces; direction arithmetic in
    face-GAN spaces famously yielded <em>smiling</em> as a vector (add it to a neutral face).
    Interpretability research treats the latent space as a scientific object — what concepts
    did the model carve? — and finds recurring structure: similar concepts in similar
    directions across independently trained models, the first evidence that these spaces
    reflect something about the data rather than the training run.
  </p>

  <h2>Steering: turning language into a control surface</h2>
  <p>
    The capability that made generation a product is <strong>conditioning</strong> — steering
    the sampler with an input. CLIP (Radford et al., 2021) learned a shared embedding space by
    training on hundreds of millions of image–caption pairs, so that an image and its
    description land at nearby points; a diffusion model conditioned on CLIP embeddings (or, in
    later systems, cross-attention against text encoders) converts a sentence into a target
    region of image space. The prompt became a programming language: subjects, styles,
    composition, even camera optics are steerable in natural language. For language models the
    equivalent is instruction tuning and RLHF — fine-tuning the raw predictor on demonstrations
    and preference ratings until it follows instructions — the difference between "a text
    engine" and "an assistant". Steering is also where failure lives: prompts can be injected
    (hidden text in an image telling a vision model to ignore instructions), and alignment
    problems (the alignment chapter's subject) are steering failures with consequences.
  </p>

  <h2>Applications, deepfakes and the provenance race</h2>
  <p>
    The application map has quietly become the economy's map. <strong>Science</strong>:
    generative models propose candidate molecules and protein structures for screening —
    generation as hypothesis engine, with diffusion models now generating protein backbones
    and crystal structures. <strong>Creative industry</strong>: concept art, storyboarding,
    in-painting, dubbing and voice cloning — the displacement-versus-augmentation debate is
    live and both effects are documented across different task types. <strong>The shadow:
    deepfakes</strong> — voice cloning has enabled documented multi-million-dollar fraud (the
    2024 case of a finance employee in Hong Kong conned out of roughly 25 million dollars by a
    video call of deepfaked colleagues), and detection lags generation permanently, because
    detectors train on yesterday's generators. That asymmetry is why the durable defence is
    provenance rather than detection: C2PA content credentials — cryptographic signed metadata
    attached at capture or generation — let consumers check where an image came from, the
    same trust-chain logic the cryptography chapter applied to certificates.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "detectors will catch deepfakes, so the problem is solved."
    Detection is an arms race the generators keep winning (every published detector is
    eventually evaded by the next generation), which is why serious institutions — newsrooms,
    banks, election bodies — build process defences (verification of the requester, out-of-band
    confirmation, signed provenance) instead of trusting classifier verdicts on pixels.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the model understands what it generates." A diffusion
  model has no referent for "dog" beyond the statistics of pixels labelled with that word;
  an autoregressive model predicts tokens, not truths — which is exactly why
  hallucination is not a bug to patch but a property of the objective (plausible continuations
  are the training target, and the plausible can be false). Whether grounded understanding
  emerges at scale is the live research question; assuming it has already arrived is how
  confident systems get trusted with consequential work.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Evaluation is unsettled.</strong> Likelihood (how well the model assigns
    probability to held-out data) is principled but incomparable across model families and
    famously uninformative for GANs; sample-quality proxies (FID — Fréchet distance between
    feature distributions of real and generated images — and CLIP score) drive leaderboards
    but reward the proxy. Human preference ratings are the gold standard and the bottleneck.
    The deeper issue is that "good generation" is task-relative: a model that excels at
    aesthetic photos may fail at correct anatomy or legible text, and the field has no single
    axis. Video, the current frontier (Sora-class systems), inherits all of this plus temporal
    consistency — objects persisting across seconds — which is a research problem in its own
    right.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The theory gaps.</strong> Why do diffusion samplers as short as 20 steps work when
    the training formulation assumes hundreds (the answer involves the model learning a
    probability-flow ODE, and distillation methods like consistency models compress the
    trajectory further)? Why does next-token prediction at scale produce reasoning-like
    behaviour — and where does it break (multi-step arithmetic, novel formal systems)? Why do
    scaling laws hold across modalities? Each is a live question where practice ran ahead of
    theory; the honest expert position is to hold the empirical regularities firmly and the
    explanations loosely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Policy as architecture.</strong> Training-data copyright litigation is the field's
    live constitutional question (fair use claims tested in US and UK courts, licensing markets
    emerging in parallel), and its technical interfaces are being built now: opt-out registries,
    robots-style signals for training crawlers, compensation models for creators. The
    generative chapter of AI therefore closes where the ethics chapter began: the design
    choices — what is collected, what is consented, what is signed — are being made once, at
    infrastructure scale, and every user of these tools is downstream of them.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goodfellow et al., NeurIPS (2014) GANs; Ho et al., NeurIPS (2020) diffusion; Ramesh et al. (DALL·E); Radford et al., CLIP (2021).</p>
</TopicPage>
